1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:13,565
In December 2013, North Korean television showed a meeting of the ruling party. Guards stepped forward, took a senior official by the arms, and led him out of the hall.

2
00:00:14,665 --> 00:00:29,236
The man was Jang Song-thaek, the uncle of the young leader, Kim Jong-un, and widely seen as the second most powerful figure in the country. Days later, state media announced that he had been executed as a traitor.

3
00:00:30,336 --> 00:00:45,333
That same winter, a United Nations commission was finishing a year-long investigation into North Korea. It estimated that between eighty and one hundred and twenty thousand people were held in political prison camps.

4
00:00:46,433 --> 00:00:59,788
Kim Jong-un has ruled North Korea since 2011. He has built nuclear weapons, met an American president and sent soldiers to fight in Europe. This is what is documented about him.

5
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:15,001
North Korea was founded in 1948, after Korea was divided at the end of the Second World War. Its first leader was Kim Jong-un's grandfather, Kim Il-sung.

6
00:01:16,101 --> 00:01:30,117
In 1950, Kim Il-sung invaded the South and started the Korean War. It ended in 1953 with an armistice, not a peace treaty. The peninsula is still divided.

7
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:41,927
Kim Il-sung built the state around his own family. Propaganda calls them the Paektu bloodline, after a sacred mountain, and teaches citizens to revere them.

8
00:01:43,027 --> 00:01:56,957
When Kim Il-sung died in 1994, his son Kim Jong-il took over. He ruled through a famine that killed hundreds of thousands of people, and he built the country's first nuclear bombs.

9
00:01:59,157 --> 00:02:14,667
Kim Jong-un was born on the eighth of January, in 1982, 1983 or 1984. Even his birth year is uncertain. He was one of Kim Jong-il's younger sons.

10
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:26,497
As a teenager, he was reportedly sent to school in Bern, Switzerland, under a false name. Classmates later remembered a shy boy who loved basketball.

11
00:02:27,597 --> 00:02:42,061
His older half-brother, Kim Jong-nam, had once been seen as a possible heir, but he fell from favour. In 2010, Kim Jong-un was suddenly made a four-star general, with no military experience.

12
00:02:43,161 --> 00:02:53,764
On the seventeenth of December 2011, his father died. Within two weeks, Kim Jong-un was named supreme commander of the armed forces.

13
00:02:54,864 --> 00:03:05,168
The outside world knew almost nothing about him. Many experts predicted that older generals would rule from behind the scenes, or that the regime would crack.

14
00:03:07,368 --> 00:03:20,040
Instead, he made his power absolute. In his first years he removed many of the senior officials he had inherited. South Korean intelligence reported that some of them were executed.

15
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:31,508
The most dramatic case was his uncle. State media called Jang a traitor and announced that he had been executed straight after a special military trial.

16
00:03:32,608 --> 00:03:44,277
In February 2017, his half-brother Kim Jong-nam, who lived in exile, was killed at Kuala Lumpur airport with VX, a banned chemical weapon.

17
00:03:45,377 --> 00:03:53,569
South Korea and the United States said the North Korean state was behind the killing. North Korea denied any role.

18
00:03:54,669 --> 00:04:05,037
Two young women were charged with his murder. They said they thought they were taking part in a television prank. Neither was convicted of murder, and both were freed.

19
00:04:07,237 --> 00:04:20,507
His father had carried out two nuclear tests. Kim Jong-un carried out four more between 2013 and 2017, each in defiance of the UN Security Council.

20
00:04:21,607 --> 00:04:35,367
The largest, in September 2017, was claimed by North Korea to be a hydrogen bomb. That November, it tested a missile that analysts said could reach the whole of the United States.

21
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:50,824
In 2013 he announced a policy of building nuclear weapons and the economy side by side. Many analysts believe he sees the bomb as the ultimate guarantee that his regime will survive.

22
00:04:51,924 --> 00:05:03,252
The UN Security Council answered with some of the toughest sanctions it has ever imposed, banning many of North Korea's main exports and capping its oil imports.

23
00:05:05,452 --> 00:05:22,049
In February 2014, a UN Commission of Inquiry led by the Australian judge Michael Kirby published its report. It drew on public testimony from more than eighty witnesses and hundreds of confidential interviews.

24
00:05:23,149 --> 00:05:33,283
Its conclusion was stark. The gravity, scale and nature of these violations reveal a State that does not have any parallel in the contemporary world.

25
00:05:34,383 --> 00:05:48,932
The commission found that crimes against humanity had been committed, including murder, enslavement, torture, imprisonment and knowingly causing prolonged starvation. Many took place in the political prison camps.

26
00:05:50,032 --> 00:05:59,717
It found that whole families could be sent to the camps for one relative's offence, and that people forcibly returned from China faced harsh punishment.

27
00:06:00,817 --> 00:06:17,564
The commission wrote to Kim Jong-un, warning that he himself might be held responsible, and urged a referral to the International Criminal Court. North Korea rejected the report as lies. As of 2025, no referral has happened.

28
00:06:18,664 --> 00:06:35,112
In 2016, an American student, Otto Warmbier, was sentenced to fifteen years of hard labour for allegedly trying to take a propaganda poster. He was sent home in a coma in 2017, and died days later.

29
00:06:37,312 --> 00:06:48,192
Then, in 2018, he changed course. In April he stepped across the border to meet South Korea's president, Moon Jae-in, in front of the cameras.

30
00:06:49,292 --> 00:07:03,820
In June 2018, in Singapore, he met Donald Trump. It was the first meeting between a sitting American president and a North Korean leader. They signed a short, vague statement on denuclearisation.

31
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:15,395
A second summit, in Hanoi in 2019, broke up without a deal. A brief meeting at the border followed that summer, and then the talks faded away.

32
00:07:16,495 --> 00:07:28,121
He gave up none of his weapons. Critics say the summits handed him prestige and time. Supporters of the talks argue that they lowered the danger of war, at least for a while.

33
00:07:30,321 --> 00:07:42,183
When Covid arrived in 2020, he sealed the borders almost completely. Even trade with China nearly stopped. UN experts warned of serious food shortages.

34
00:07:43,283 --> 00:07:50,792
That June, North Korea blew up the liaison office it had shared with South Korea since 2018.

35
00:07:51,892 --> 00:08:03,604
In 2022 it launched more missiles than in any year before, and passed a law allowing the use of nuclear weapons first, if the leadership was threatened.

36
00:08:04,704 --> 00:08:15,819
In January 2024, he declared South Korea the country's principal enemy and dropped the long-standing official goal of peaceful reunification.

37
00:08:16,919 --> 00:08:29,761
He turned instead to Russia. In June 2024, Vladimir Putin visited Pyongyang, and the two countries signed a treaty promising each other military help if attacked.

38
00:08:30,861 --> 00:08:47,288
Late in 2024, according to South Korean, Ukrainian and Western intelligence, thousands of North Korean soldiers were sent to fight for Russia against Ukraine. In April 2025, Pyongyang confirmed it.

39
00:08:49,488 --> 00:09:04,165
As of 2025, Kim Jong-un had ruled for more than thirteen years. A young daughter often appears at his side, and South Korea's intelligence service has suggested she may be his chosen successor.

40
00:09:05,265 --> 00:09:17,127
Many analysts describe him as ruthless but calculating. His purges, his missiles and his summits can all be read as serving one goal: keeping his family in power.

41
00:09:18,227 --> 00:09:27,720
Around twenty-six million people live under that system. They cannot vote him out, read foreign news freely, or leave the country without permission.

42
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:42,921
Kim Jong-un has never faced a court. But in 2014 the United Nations wrote down his country's crimes in detail, and addressed its warning to him by name. That record will outlast him.
